        "topic": "Set Operations",
        "question_type": "Theory",
        "exam_year": "2014",
        "question_text": "<p>Given the sets $A = \\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}$ and $B = \\{3, 4, 5, 6\\}$, find:<br>(i) $A \\cup B$<br>(ii) $A \\cap B$<br>(iii) $A - B$</p>",
        "question_image": null,
        "answer_image": null,
        "explanation": "<p>(i) The union of sets $A$ and $B$, denoted $A \\cup B$, includes all elements that are in $A$, in $B$, or in both. Thus, $A \\cup B = \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\\}$.<br>(ii) The intersection of sets $A$ and $B$, denoted $A \\cap B$, includes only the elements common to both $A$ and $B$. Thus, $A \\cap B = \\{3, 4\\}$.<br>(iii) The difference $A - B$ includes elements in $A$ that are not in $B$. Thus, $A - B = \\{1, 2\\}$.</p>",
        "reference_passage": null,
