        "topic": "Real Number Properties",
        "question_type": "Theory",
        "exam_year": "2014",
        "question_text": "<p>Prove that the sum of any two rational numbers is a rational number.</p>",
        "question_image": null,
        "answer_image": null,
        "explanation": "<p>Let $a$ and $b$ be two rational numbers. By definition, $a = \\frac{p}{q}$ and $b = \\frac{r}{s}$, where $p$, $q$, $r$, and $s$ are integers, and $q \\neq 0$, $s \\neq 0$.<br>The sum of $a$ and $b$ is:<br>$a + b = \\frac{p}{q} + \\frac{r}{s} = \\frac{ps + rq}{qs}$.<br>Since $ps + rq$ and $qs$ are integers and $qs \\neq 0$, $a + b$ is a rational number.</p>",
        "reference_passage": null,
        "novel": null,
        "comments_count": 0,
